\begin_layout Title
Chapter 3: Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Need to complete: Corollary 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer Theory IV: Revealed Preference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.8
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Expenditure Minimization Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this subsection, we study the following 
\emph on
expenditure minimization problem
\emph default
 (EMP) for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Also, we implicitly assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\succsim0$
\end_inset

; these restrictions rule out only uninteresting situaitons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(81).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
Expenditure Minimization Problem (EMP)
\series default
 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\min & p\cdot x\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x\geq0\ \mbox{and}\ x\succsim x^{*}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Whereas the UMP computes the maximal level of utility that can be obtained
 given wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, the EMP computes the minimal level of wealth required to reach a bundle
 at least as good as 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The EMP is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 problem to the UMP.
 It captures the same aim of efficient use of the consumer's purchasing
 power while reversing the roles of objective function and constraint.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Geometrically, the optimal consumption bundle of the EMP is the point in
 the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\succsim x^{*}\}$
\end_inset

 that lies on the lowest possible budget line associated with the price
 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(82).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 be a set and 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 a real valued function defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 An element 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A$
\end_inset

 is a 
\series bold
minimizer of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset


\series default
 if for each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y)\geq f(x)$
\end_inset

.
 The set of minimizers of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\arg\min_{x\in A}f(x)$
\end_inset

, or alternatively 
\begin_inset Formula $\arg\min\left\{ f(x):x\in A\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
As with the UMP, when 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 a solution to the EMP exists under very general conditions.
 The constraint set merely needs to be nonempty; that is, the upper contour
 set of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 needs to be nonempty.
 From now on, we assume that this is so.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now proceed to study the optimal consumption vector and the value function
 of the EMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(83).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 represented by a utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
Hicksian (compensated) demand correspondence
\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
h: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{\mathbb{R}}\\
 & (p,x^{*}) & \mapsto & h(p,x^{*})\equiv{\displaystyle \arg\min_{x\geq0,x\succsim x^{*}}}\ p\cdot x
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Proposition 21 describes the formal relationship between the EMP and the
 UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(21).

\series default
 
\emph on
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation defined on
 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 .
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)\iff\left[p\cdot x^{*}=w\ \wedge\ x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation defined on
 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is locally non-satiated, then Walras' Law holds, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\notin h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x^{\prime}\ge0,\ x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}:p\cdot x^{\prime}<p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

, which is an open set by definition.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

 is an open set, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0:B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x^{\prime})\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x^{\prime}):y\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B_{\epsilon}^{X}(x^{\prime})\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mbox{Int}(B(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}\implies y\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, our claim is true, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

 and 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\notin C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x^{\prime}\in B(p,w):x^{\prime}\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in U(\succ,x^{*})$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
.
 By Corollary 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 are open in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is open, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \alpha\in(0,1):\alpha x^{\prime}\in U(\succ,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\equiv\alpha x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}=0$
\end_inset

, then we have 
\begin_inset Formula $0\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot0<w=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 On the other hand, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\gneq x^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 So, for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}<p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}<p\cdot x^{\prime}\leq w=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this also contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in C^{\succsim}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Expenditure Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and required level 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, the value of the EMP is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(84).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
expenditure function
\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

, is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
e: & \mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & (p,x^{*}) & \mapsto & e(p,x^{*})\equiv{\displaystyle \min_{x\geq0,x\succsim x^{*}}}\ p\cdot x
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\emph default
The value for any 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is any solution to the EMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Proposition 21 allows us to make an important connection between the expenditure
 function and the indirect utility function developed in Section 3.5.6.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(5).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that induces a single valued choice correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is single valued and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})=p\cdot h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x(p,w))=w$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})=x(p,e(p,x^{*}))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)=h(p,x(p,w))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim0$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e(p,x(p,w))=w\ \mbox{and}\ V^{u}(p,e(p,x(p,w)))=u(x(p,w))
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in\arg\max_{x\in B(p,w)}u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 These conditions imply that for a fixed price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\bar{p},\cdot)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(\bar{p},\cdot)$
\end_inset

 are inverses to one another.
 There is a direct correspondence between the properties of the expenditure
 function and the indirect utility function.
 They both capture the same underlying features of the consumer's choice
 problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The result in Proposition 22 descrives the basic properties of the expenditure
 function.
 It parallels Proposition 20's characterization of the properties of the
 indirect utility function for the UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(22).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Homogeneous of degree one in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, i.e., for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\alpha p,x^{*})=\alpha e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Non-decreasing in each price component.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e., if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)\geq e(p,y)$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)>e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The constraint set of the EMP is unchanged when prices change.
 Thus, for any scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

, minimizing 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha p)\cdot x$
\end_inset

 on this set leads to the same optimal consumption bundles as minimizing
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 Letting 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 be optimal in both circumstances, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\alpha p,x^{*})=\alpha p\cdot x^{*}=\alpha e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To show that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)$
\end_inset

 is non-decreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 , suppose that price vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 have 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}\geq p_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}^{\prime}=p_{k}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq l$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\gneq p$
\end_inset

.
 We also hold 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 fixed at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 be an optimizing vector in the EMP for prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{\prime},\bar{x})=p^{\prime}\cdot x^{\prime}\geq p\cdot x^{\prime}\geq e(p,\bar{x})$
\end_inset

, where the latter inequality follows from the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\bar{x})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)\geq e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x)$
\end_inset

.
 By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim x\succsim y\implies x^{*}\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}\geq e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)>e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)\leq e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

 denote optimal consumption bundles for required levels 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, respectively, where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<p\cdot x^{*}\leq p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose not.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=0$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $0\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $0\succsim x\succ y\implies0\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 By definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,y)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot0\geq p\cdot y$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $y=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $0\succ0$
\end_inset

, but this is a contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim x\succ y\implies x^{*}\succ y$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in U(\succ,y)$
\end_inset

.
 By Corollary 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 are open in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,y)$
\end_inset

 is open, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \alpha\in(0,1):\alpha x^{*}\in U(\succ,y)$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\equiv\alpha x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\neq0$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\gneq\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

, so for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\tilde{x}<p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}\succ y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\tilde{x}<p\cdot x^{*}\leq p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in h(p,y)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x)>e(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
