\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,x^{*})\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is convex, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq h(p,x^{*}),\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{*}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{*}\ \wedge\ x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}\implies\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq U(\succsim,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is convex, then 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succsim,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is convex, so 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\in U(\succsim,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha p\cdot x+(1-\alpha)p\cdot x^{\prime}=p\cdot x=p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume next that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ (p,x^{*})\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is not single-valued.
 So, take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq h(p,x^{*}):x\neq x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{*}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}=\alpha x+(1-\alpha)x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}=p\cdot x=p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, by strict convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq U(\succsim,x^{\prime})\implies x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\in U(\succ,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Also, by transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{\prime}\succsim0\implies x^{\prime\prime}\succ0$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 By Corollary 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 are open.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is open, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \beta\in(0,1):\beta x^{\prime\prime}\in U(\succ,x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta x^{\prime\prime}\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{\prime}\succsim x^{*}\implies\beta x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime\prime}\gneq\beta x^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot(\beta x^{\prime\prime})<p\cdot x^{\prime\prime}=p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta x^{\prime\prime}\succ x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\left(\beta x^{\prime\prime}\right)<p\cdot x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 But this contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using Proposition 21, Corollary 5 also related the Hicksian and Walrasian
 demand correspondences as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h(p,x^{*})=x(p,e(p,x^{*}))\ \mbox{and}\ x(p,w)=h(p,x(p,w))
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The first of these relations explains the use of the term 
\emph on
compensated demand correspondence
\emph default
 to describe 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

: As prices vary, 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 gives precisely the level of demand that would arise if the consumer's
 wealth were simultaneously adjust to keep her utility level at 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 This type of wealth compensation is known as 
\emph on
Hicksian wealth compensation
\emph default
.
 For example, in 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

, let the consumer's initial situation be the price-wealth pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and then have prices change to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{\prime}=p_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}^{\prime}>p_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The Hicksian wealth compensation is the amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w_{Hicks}=e(p^{\prime},x^{*})-w$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 keeps the consumer's utility level fixed as prices change, in contrast
 with the Walrasian demand function, which keeps money wealth fixed bu allows
 utility to vary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As with the value functions of the EMP and UMP, the relations of the corresponde
nces in Corollary 5 allow us to develop tight linkage between the properties
 of the Hicksian demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 and the Walrasian demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Hicksian Demand and the Compensated Law of Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An important property of Hicksian demand is that it satisfies the 
\emph on
compensated law of demand
\emph default
: Demand and price move in opposite directions for price changes that are
 accompanied by Hicksian wealth compensation.
 In Proposition 24, we prove this fact for the case of single-valued Hicksian
 demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(24).

\series default
\emph on
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is single valued, then it satisfies the compensated law of demand.
 That is, for any two price vectors, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\gg0$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[p^{\prime}-p\right]\cdot\left[h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-h(p,x^{*})\right]\leq0
\]

\end_inset

with strict inequality whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p^{\prime},x^{*})\neq h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For any 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the EMP, and so it achieves a lower expenditure at prices
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 than any other budle that offers a level of at least 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{\prime}\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})\leq p^{\prime}\cdot h(p,x^{*})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot h(p,x^{*})\leq p\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})
\]

\end_inset

Now, consider
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[p^{\prime}-p\right]\cdot\left[h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-h(p,x^{*})\right] & = & p^{\prime}\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-p^{\prime}h(p,x^{*})-p\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})+p\cdot h(p,x^{*})\\
 & = & \left[p^{\prime}\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-p^{\prime}\cdot h(p,x^{*})\right]+\left[p\cdot h(p,x^{*})-p\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-p^{\prime}\cdot h(p,x^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot h(p,x^{*})-p\cdot h(p^{\prime},x^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{\prime}-p)\cdot(h(p^{\prime},x^{*})-h(p,x^{*}))\leq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One immediate implication of Proposition 24 is that for compensated demand,
 own-price effects are nonpositive.
 In particular, if only 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 changes, Proposition 24 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{l}^{\prime}-p_{l})[h_{l}(p^{\prime},x^{*})-h_{l}(p,x^{*})]\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 The comparable statement is 
\emph on
not 
\emph default
true for Walrasian demand.
 Walrasian demand need not satisfy the law of demand.
 For example, the demand for a good can decrease when its price falls.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here and in preceding subsections, we have derived several basic properties
 of the Walrasian and Hicksian demand functions, the indirect utility function,
 and the expenditure functions.
 We now continue our exploration of results flowing from the UMP and the
 EMP.
 The investigation in the next subsections concern three relationships:
 that between the Hicksian demand function and the expenditure function,
 that between the Hicksian and Walrasian demand functions, and that between
 the Walrasian demand function and the indirect utility function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Hicksian Demand and the Expenditure Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From knowledge of the Hicksian demand function, the expenditure function
 readily be calculated as 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})=p\cdot h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 The important result shown in Proposition 25 establishes a more significant
 link between the two concepts that runs in the opposite direction.
 Given the expenditure function, we can calculate the consumer's Hicksian
 demand function simply by differentiating.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(25).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\cdot,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is differentiable with respect to prices and for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial e(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{l}}=h_{l}(p,x^{*})
\]

\end_inset

or in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}e(p,x^{*})=h(p,x^{*})^{T}
\]

\end_inset


\shape default
\emph default
[In alternative gradient notation, this equality is 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{p}e(p,x^{*})=h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

].
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
We present two methods to proving this proposition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Method 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is differentiable, then the function 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\mapsto\varphi(p^{\prime})\equiv p^{\prime}\cdot h(p,x^{*})-e(p^{\prime},x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and has minimum at 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}=p$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}\varphi(p)=h(p,x^{*})^{T}-D_{p}e(p,x^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Method 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The expenditure function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e(p,x^{*})={\displaystyle \min_{h\succsim x^{*}}\ p\cdot h\ ={\displaystyle \min_{u(h)\ge u(x^{*})}}\ p\cdot h}
\]

\end_inset

while the Lagrangian of the expenditure minimization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L(p,x^{*},h)=p\cdot h+\lambda(u(h)-u(x^{*}))
\]

\end_inset

By the Envelope Theorem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
D_{p}e(p,x^{*}) & = & D_{p}L(p,x^{*},h(p,x^{*}))\\
D_{p}e(p,x^{*}) & = & h(p,x^{*})^{T}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The idea behind the two proofs is the same: If we are at an optimum in the
 EMP, the changes in demand caused by price changes have no first-order
 effect on the consumer's expenditure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Proposition 26 summarizes several properties of the price derivatives of
 the Hicksian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 that are implied by Proposition 25 [properties (i) to (iii)].
 It also records one additional fact about these derivatives [property (iv)].
\end_layout

