\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Hicksian and Walrasian Demand Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Although the Hicksian demand function is not directly observable (it has
 the consumer's ideal consumption bundle as an argument), we now show that
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 can nevertheless be compute from the observable Walrasian demand function
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 (its arguments are all observable in principle).
 This important reslut, known as the 
\emph on
Slutsky equation
\emph default
, means that the properties in Proposition 26 translate into restrictions
 on the observable Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(27) (The Slutsky Equation).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the aossciated choice correspondence is single-valued with
 demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

, and each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{l,k\}\subseteq\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial h_{l}(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{k}}=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,w)=S_{lk}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

or equivalently, in matrix notation,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}h(p,x^{*})=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{T}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\equiv x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Consider a consumer facing the price-wealth pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the wealth level must satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $w=e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\equiv x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 By Corollary 5, we that 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{l}(p,x^{*})=x_{l}(p,e(p,x^{*}))$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial h_{l}(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{k}}=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,e(p,x^{*}))}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,e(p,x^{*}))}{\partial w}\frac{\partial e(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{k}}
\]

\end_inset

By Proposition 25,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial e(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{k}}=h_{k}(p,x^{*})
\]

\end_inset

Finally, since 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})=w$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{k}(p,x^{*})=x_{k}(p,e(p,x^{*}))=x_{k}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial h_{l}(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{k}}=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}h(p,x(p,w))$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}(p,\bar{w})$
\end_inset

 and the Hicksian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{l}(p,x(\bar{p},\bar{w}))$
\end_inset

 for good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

, holding other prices fixed at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}_{-l}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the two demand functions are equal when 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=\bar{p}_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 The Slutsky equation describes the relationship between the slopes of these
 two functions at price 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 One possibility is that the slope of the Walrasian demand curve at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}_{l}$
\end_inset

 is less negative than the slope of the Hicksian demand curve at that price.
 From inspection of the Slutsky equation, this corresponds to a situation
 where good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is a normal good at 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bar{p},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 increases above 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}_{l}$
\end_inset

, we must increase the consumer's wealth if we are to keep her at the same
 level of utility offered by bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is normal, its demand falls by more in the absense of this compensation.
 The other possibility is when good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 is an inferior good.
 In this case, the Walrasian demand curve has a more negative slope than
 the Hicksian curve.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary*

\series bold
\shape up
(6).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that the associated choice correspondence is single-valued with
 demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Corollary 6 is a direct result of Propositions 26 and 27.
 Proposition 27 implies that the matrix of price derivatives 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 of the Hicksian demand function is equal to the matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S(p,w)=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
s_{11}(p,w) & \cdots & s_{1L}(p,w)\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
s_{L1}(p,w) & \cdots & s_{LL}(p,w)
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{lk}(p,w)=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

This matrix is known as the 
\emph on
Slutsky substitution matrix
\emph default
.
 Note, in particular, that 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is directly computable from knowledge of the (observable) Walrasian demand
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Since Proposition 26 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 is symmetric, then 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric.
\end_layout

