\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Corollary 7 is a direct result of Propositions 26, 27, and 28.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

, Propositions 26 and 27 imply that when demand is generated from preference
 maximization, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 must possess the following three properties: it must be 
\emph on
negative semi-definite, symmetric, and satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Section 3.4, the Slutsky substitution matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 was shown to be the matrix of compensated demand derivatives arising from
 a different form of wealth compensation, the so-called 
\emph on
Slutsky wealth compensation
\emph default
.
 Instead of varying wealth to keep utility fixed, as we do here, Slutsky
 compensation adjusts wealth so that the initial consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 is just affordable at the new prices.
 Thus, we have the remarkable conclusion that the 
\emph on
derivative of the Hicksian demand function
\emph default
 
\emph on
is equal to the derivative of this alternative Slutsky compensated demand
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can understand this result as follows: Suppose we are at initial position
 
\begin_inset Formula $(\bar{p},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}=x(\bar{p},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

.
 As we change prices to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, we want to change wealth in order to compensate for the wealth effect
 arising from this price change.
 In principle, the compensation can be down in two ways.
 By changing wealth by amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w_{Slutsky}=p^{\prime}\cdot x(\bar{p},\bar{w})-\bar{w}$
\end_inset

, we leave the consumer just able to afford her initial bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Alternatively, we can change wealth by amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w_{Hicks}=e(p^{\prime},\bar{x})-\bar{w}$
\end_inset

 to keep her utility level unchanged.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta w_{Hicks}\leq\Delta w_{Slutsky}$
\end_inset

, and the inequality will, in general, be strict for any discrete change.
 But because 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla_{p}e(\bar{p},\bar{x})=h(\bar{p},\bar{x})=x(\bar{p},\bar{w})$
\end_inset

, these two compensations are 
\emph on
identical
\emph default
 for a differential price change starting at 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{p}$
\end_inset

.
 Intuitively, this is due to the same fact that led to Proposition 25: For
 a differential change in prices, the total effect on the expenditure required
 to achieve indifference to bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 (the Hicksian compensation level), is simply the direct effect of the price
 change, assuming that the consumption bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 does not change.
 But this is precisely the calculation done for Slutsky compensation.
 Hence, the derivatives of the compensated demand functions that arise from
 these two compensation mechansims are the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Furthermore, we can compare the implications of the preference-based approach
 to consumer demand with those derived using a choice-based approach built
 on the weak axiom.
 In the previous section, we concluded that if 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the weak axiom (plus homogeneity of degree zero and Walras' Law),
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite with 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover, we argued that except when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

, demand satisfying the weak axiom need not have a symmetric Slutsky substitutio
n matrix.
 Therefore, the results here tell us that the restrictions imposed on demand
 in the preference-based approach are stronger than those arising in the
 choice-based thoery built on the weak axiom.
 In fact, it is impossible to find preferences that rationalize demand when
 the substitution matrix is not symmetric.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Walrasian Demand and the Indirect Utility Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have seen that the minimizing vector of the EMP, 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

, is the derivative with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 of the EMP's value function 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 The exactly analogous statement for the UMP does not hold.
 The Walrasian demand, an ordinal concept, cannot equal the price derivative
 of the indirect utility function, which is not invariant to increasing
 transformations of utility.
 But with a small correciton in which we normalize the derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 by the marginal utility of wealth, it holds true.
 This proposition called 
\emph on
Roy's Identity
\emph default
, is the parallel result to Proposition 25 for the demand and value functions
 of the UMP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(29) (Roy's Identitiy)
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 represented by a continuously differentiable utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that the indirect utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}$
\end_inset

 is differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{l}(p,w)=-\frac{\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}}{\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}}
\]

\end_inset

or in matrix notation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w)=-\frac{1}{\nabla_{w}V^{u}(p,w)}\nabla_{p}V^{u}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\equiv x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}=u(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Observe that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{u}=V^{u}(p,e(p,x^{*}))$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

(Method 1): Applying the chain rule and differentiating with respect to
 price of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0=\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,e(p,x^{*}))}{\partial p_{l}}+\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,e(p,x^{*}))}{\partial w}\frac{\partial e_{l}(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $w=e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial e(p,x^{*})}{\partial p_{l}}=h_{l}(p,x^{*})=x_{l}(p,e(p,x^{*}))=x_{l}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, then rearranging terms we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{l}(p,w)=-\frac{\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}}{\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

(Method 2): Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is differentiable and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 By the chain rule, we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}={\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}}\frac{\partial u(x(p,w))}{\partial x_{k}}\frac{\partial x_{k}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}
\]

\end_inset

Substituting for 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\partial u(x(p,w))}{\partial x_{k}}$
\end_inset

 using the first-order conditions for the UMP, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}} & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{L}}\lambda p_{k}\frac{\partial x_{k}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}}\\
 & = & \lambda x_{l}(p,w)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}p_{k}(\nicefrac{\partial x_{k}(p,w)}{\partial p_{l}})=-x_{l}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 (Proposition 2.E.2).
 Finally, we have already argued that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\nicefrac{\partial V^{u}(p,w)}{\partial w}$
\end_inset

; use of this fact yields the result.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Proposition 29 provides a substantial payoff.
 Walrasian demand is much easier to compute from indirect than from direct
 utility.
 To derive 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 from the indirect utility function, no more than the calculation of derivatives
 is involved; no system of first-order condition equations needs to be solved.
 Thus, it may often be more convenient to express tastes in indirect utility
 form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now summarize the connections we have made in this section.
 Starting from a given utility function in the UMP or the EMP, we can derive
 the optimal consumption bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

 and the value functions 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{u}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, we can go back and forth between the value functions and the
 demand functions of the two problems using relationships (1) and (2).
 Finally, we have seen that the demand vector for each problem can be calculated
 from its value function and the derivatives of the Hicksian demand function
 can be calculated from the observable Walrasian demand using Slutsky's
 equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Integrability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If a continuously differentiable demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is generated by rational preferences, then we have seen that it must be
 homogeneous of degree zero, satisfy Walras' law, and have a substitution
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 that is symmetric and negative semidefinite at all 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 We now pose the reverse question: 
\emph on
If we observe a demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 that has these properties, can we find preferences that rationalize 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\cdot)$
\end_inset

?
\emph default
 The answer is yes; these conditions are difficient for the existence of
 rational generating preferences.
 This problem is known as the 
\emph on
integrability problem
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
(16) (Integrability Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{B}^{W},C)$
\end_inset

 be a single-valued Walrasian choice structure satisfying Walras' law and
 whose associated demand funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is differentiable.
 If the partial derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfy some 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

technical additional conditions,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 then symmetry and negative semi-definiteness of the Slutsky matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at each pair of prices and wealth is sufficient for the existence of a
 utility funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $C=C^{u}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{u}$
\end_inset

 is the choice correspondence associated with the preference represented
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The Integrability Theorem was briefly mentioned in class, but there was
 no in-depth discussion.
 Moreover, the proof is technical and beyond the scope of what we learned
 in class.
 As a result, the proof will not be presented in these notes.
 For more information on the Integrability Theorem, see Section 3.H in Mas-Colell
 (1995).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
If 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP and some boundedness conditions, then there exists a utility
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $C=C^{u}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{u}$
\end_inset

 is the choice correspondence associated with the preference represented
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 rationalizes choice, i.e., the preference, represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, induces the choices 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the preference represents the choice structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.5.9
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose that Walrasian demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and is such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}(p,w) & \equiv & a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\\
x_{2}(p,w) & \equiv & d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Prove 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Calculate the Slutsky matrix at some 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Provide the restricitons on the demand parameters that are implied by utility
 maximization.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot x=p_{1}x_{1}(p,w)+p_{2}x_{2}(p,w)+p_{3}x_{3}(p,w)=w
\]

\end_inset

Since we have the expressions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

, then we can solve for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 using the equation above.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{3}(p,w) & = & \frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}x_{1}(p,w)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}x_{2}(p,w)\\
 & = & \frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
x_{1}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{1}(p,w)\\
x_{2}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}=d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}=x_{2}(p,w)\\
x_{3}(\alpha p,\alpha w)=\frac{\alpha w}{\alpha p_{3}}-\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+c\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)-\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{\alpha p_{1}}{\alpha p_{3}}+f\frac{\alpha p_{2}}{\alpha p_{3}}\right)\\
=\frac{w}{p_{3}}-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}\left(a+b\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+c\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)-\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\left(d+e\frac{p_{1}}{p_{3}}+f\frac{p_{2}}{p_{3}}\right)=x_{3}(p,w)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ (p,w)\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}:x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $lk$
\end_inset

 component of the Slutsky matrix associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{lk}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, is defined by the following equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{lk}(p,w)=\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial p_{k}}+\frac{\partial x_{l}(p,w)}{\partial w}x_{k}(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

Take 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

.
 Using the equation above, we calculate each component of the Slutsky matrix.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
S_{11}(p,w)=\frac{b}{p_{3}}\\
S_{12}(p,w)=\frac{c}{p_{3}}\\
S_{13}(p,w)=-\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[bp_{1}+cp_{2}\right]\\
S_{21}(p,w)=\frac{e}{p_{3}}\\
S_{22}(p,w)=\frac{f}{p_{3}}\\
S_{23}(p,w)=-\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[ep_{1}+fp_{2}\right]\\
S_{31}(p,w)=-\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[bp_{1}+ep_{2}\right]\\
S_{32}(p,w)=-\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[cp_{1}+fp_{2}\right]\\
S_{33}(p,w)=\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[p_{1}p_{2}(c+e)+\frac{1}{p_{3}}\left(bp_{1}^{2}+fp_{2}^{2}\right)\right]
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Propositions 26 and 27 imply that when demand is generated from preference
 maximization, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 must possess the following three properties: it must be 
\emph on
negative semi-definite, symmetric, and satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\emph default
Now, let us consider the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

, which is obtained by deleting the 2nd row and 2nd column of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 By Proposition 28, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 must also be symmetric, we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S_{12}(p,w)=S_{21}(p,w)\iff\frac{c}{p_{3}}=\frac{e}{p_{3}}\iff c=e
\]

\end_inset

Note that we obtain the same restriction for 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{13}(p,w)=S_{31}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{23}(p,w)=S_{32}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is negative semi-definite if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $-S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is positive definite.
 
\begin_inset Formula $-S_{2}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is positive semi-definite if and only if its principal minors are all non-negat
ive.
 Hence, this leads to the following restrictions:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\left|-S_{11}(p,w)\right|\geq0\iff-\frac{b}{p_{3}}\geq0\iff b\leq0\\
\left|-S_{22}(p,w)\right|\geq0\iff-\frac{f}{p_{3}}\geq0\iff f\leq0\\
\left|-S_{2}(p,w)\right|\geq0\iff\left|\begin{array}{cc}
-\frac{b}{p_{3}} & -\frac{c}{p_{3}}\\
-\frac{e}{p_{3}} & -\frac{f}{p_{3}}
\end{array}\right|\geq0\iff bf-ce\geq0\iff bf\geq ce
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Finally, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
\frac{b}{p_{3}} & \frac{c}{p_{3}} & -\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[bp_{1}+cp_{2}\right]\\
\frac{e}{p_{3}} & \frac{f}{p_{3}} & -\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[ep_{1}+fp_{2}\right]\\
-\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[bp_{1}+ep_{2}\right] & -\frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[cp_{1}+fp_{2}\right] & \frac{1}{p_{3}^{2}}\left[p_{1}p_{2}(c+e)+\frac{1}{p_{3}}\left(bp_{1}^{2}+fp_{2}^{2}\right)\right]
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
p_{1}\\
p_{2}\\
p_{3}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0\\
0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 (Here, it would suffice to assume that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim0$
\end_inset

.) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 Prove that the money metric representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
(Important to note, we are proving the same result as in Problem 11, Section
 3.3.3.
 The only difference is that we now weaken monotonicity to local non-satiation,
 and add the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\succsim0$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a locally non-satiated continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\succsim0$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{\mathbb{R}}_{++}^{L}.$
\end_inset

 Consider the function 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

 that associates with each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 the value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m_{p}(x)\equiv{\displaystyle \min_{z\geq0,z\succsim x}}\ p\cdot z
\]

\end_inset

 We will prove 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in h(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(y)=p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, by transitivity, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\succsim x\succsim y\implies x^{*}\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}\geq p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(x\succsim y)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 By the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\neg(x\succsim y)\ \wedge\ y\succsim x\iff y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x\succsim0\implies y\succ0$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in h(p,y)$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\succsim y\succ0\implies y^{*}\succ0$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}(x)\geq m_{p}(y)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}\geq p\cdot y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, by Theorem Corollary 1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 are open.
 Now, by transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\succsim y\succ x\implies y^{*}\succ x$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{*}\in U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

 is open, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \alpha\in(0,1):\alpha y^{*}\in U(\succ,x)$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\equiv\alpha y^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\in U(\succ,x)\implies\tilde{y}\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gneq\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}\geq p\cdot y^{*}>p\cdot\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}>p\cdot\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\succ x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}>p\cdot\tilde{y}$
\end_inset

.
 But this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,x)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a monotone and continuous preference whose associated demand correspondence
 is single-valued.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the associated demand function.
 The 
\series bold
equivalent variation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 at wealth level 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset


\series default
, denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)$
\end_inset

 is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
EV^{\succsim}(p,p^{\prime},w)\equiv V^{m\left\langle \succsim,p\right\rangle }(p^{\prime},w)-V^{m\left\langle \succsim,p\right\rangle }(p,w)
\]

\end_inset

The 
\series bold
compensating variation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
